# Ignore file for the E2E backend image (e2e/docker/backend.e2e.Dockerfile).
#
# BuildKit uses "<Dockerfile name>.dockerignore" from the Dockerfile's own directory in preference
# to a .dockerignore at the root of the build context, so this file is what filters backend/ when
# scripts/build.sh runs:
#   docker buildx build -f e2e/docker/backend.e2e.Dockerfile backend
#
# Part 1 is a verbatim copy of backend/.dockerignore (scripts/preflight.sh checks every line of it
# is still present). Part 2 is the E2E addition: no secret-bearing file may reach the image.

# --- part 1: identical to backend/.dockerignore -------------------------------------------------
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.python-version
*.db
C:\*
DashboardSpecs.md
Notes.md
routes_arch.md
validation_error.txt
mongo-compose.yml
testing.py
*.pdf
db_confs/

# --- part 2: E2E additions (never bake a real credential into a test image) ---------------------
# backend/.env and backend/utilities/ai/.env are TRACKED in git and hold real AWS key ids; the
# Dockerfile also COPYs _migrations/ (a populated copy of prod_database_migrations/, which tracks
# its own .env). scripts/build.sh asserts none of them exists inside the built image.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
_migrations/.env
# agent runtime state that happens to be tracked; not application code
.claude-flow/
